{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790980500000-architect-floatfix-signoff.prompt.md; tail -220 collab/1790973728000-compiler-engineer-python-floatfix.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-02 22:40:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-03 05:40:00 +07 (+0700)\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 2f42188d-f1e5-4b5b-a212-4d0f2ee514f9 (resumed; your float-address mob session)\n\n# Task: Two sign-off questions on the float-fix implementation\n\nRole: Lead System Architect\n\nThe float-fix engineer implemented your converged ruling (with astra) — cross-host goldens are byte-identical on\nJVM/Node/Dart, the carrier is inserted at the producer, the bridge covers vm.cljc/engine.cljc scalar gates AND the\nde Bruijn executable-scalar gates your correction 2 did not name (it found Node's stack/register VMs refused\ncarrier-bearing images there, and fixed a -0.0 sign drop in both double-le-rà-hex copies). Lanes green. The\nimplementation tree: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-floatfix (branch yang-python-floatfix, uncommitted;\nreport collab/1790973728000-compiler-engineer-python-floatfix.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log in the main tree).\n\nTWO QUESTIONS need your ruling before it can land:\n\n1. DICT-KEY NORMALIZATION (the engineer's flagged decision): it added data/numeric-key — a numeric dict/set key\n   is the INTEGER when integral within ±(2^53-1), so 1, 1.0, True and -0.0/0 collapse to one key; otherwise the\n   key is float64 content. Sign off, or correct? AND the interaction: C3 ruling 6 (C3-S2, in flight in another\n   worktree) replaces numeric key normalization with reduced-rational DECIMAL-STRING keys. Does the engineer's\n   interim numeric-key survive as the C3-S2 foundation (C3-S2 then stringifies over it), or should the floatfix\n   landing drop numeric-key and leave keys untouched until C3-S2 lands? Rule the sequencing.\n2. THE BRIDGE SCOPE: the engineer reports \"generic yin code is not bridged — a float literal decoded from a row\n   on JS evaluates as a carrier; only the Python path converts back through data/float-value\". Is that an\n   acceptable reading of your correction 2 (the named gates pass and values re-enter execution only through\n   Python's seam), or must the bridge be universal (every consumer unwraps), in which case name the additional\n   surfaces for this slice?\n\nRead the implementation tree as needed. End with \"Ruling:\" — decisive, per question. Read-only; do not edit files.\n\nBegin the final response exactly with:\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS +0700>\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-02 22:25:45 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-03 05:25:45 +0700\n\nThe float-address fix is in and all three lanes are green. The cross-host goldens are byte-identical on the JVM, Node and Dart. Python floats now carry Jing's float64 carrier from the point they are produced. `dao.jing.cbor.cljc` and its fixtures are untouched, with no new tag, row shape or AST tag. Nothing is staged or committed.\n\n## Lanes (foreground-equivalent, one at a time)\nThe tool caps a foreground call at 10 minutes, so I launched each lane through the harness's background option. I kept the turn open with blocking wait calls until each one exited.\n\n| Lane | Tests | Assertions | Failures | Errors | Skips |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `bb test:clj` (ran `gen:python-antlr` and `build:yin-repl-node` first) | 2904 | 227043 | 0 | 0 | 1 (ws-project Dart peer not built) |\n| `bb test:cljs` | 2710 | 91930 | 0 | 0 | 1 (Dart peer absent at the time) |\n| `bb test:cljd` | 2665 passed | — | 0 | — | — |\n\n- **Lint:** `clj -M:kondo` reports 0 errors on the touched files. The remaining warnings in `vm.cljc` were already there. One info note remains in `render.cljc:36` (redundant `double` coercion), which I kept to avoid a reflection warning.\n- **Formatting:** `cljstyle check` is clean on all touched files.\n- **Node lane history:** the first Node run printed no summary line; the build log suggests the run stopped part-way. Two later runs exposed and fixed the de Bruijn gate described below. The final run is the one in the table.\n\n## Cross-host goldens (`test/yang/python/antlr/float_address_test.cljc`, new)\nAll goldens were computed on the JVM, and the same assertions pass on Node and Dart, so every one is byte-identical across hosts. They pin:\n- **Canonical bytes:** `{:py/float 2.0}`, `float64 1` vs integer `1`, and -0.0.\n- **Bundled prelude, standalone:** `ccd573ee…`\n- **Hook prelude:** `76e1cfe8…`\n- **Program and derivation:** the test program is `while False: pass` followed by `print(2.0, 1 + 2.0, 4 / 2, -0.0, 0.5, 1)`:\n  - A `e9ce6327…`\n  - A' `8d572c58…`\n  - the derive record `3ba9c4dd…`\n  - the bundled prelude inside A `feb8c22d…`\n- **Dict-key bytes.**\n\nThe same file also checks:\n- 1 and 1.0 have different addresses, and -0.0 keeps its sign.\n- Kind survives projection and decoding: decoding A and projecting it again gives the same address.\n- A runs on all four VMs, both as projected and after decoding from bytes.\n- The base prelude and the hook prelude contain no integral float literal, and every float literal sits inside `data/float-value`.\n\n## Changed files\n- **`lower.cljc`:** float literals become `{:py/float (cbor/float64 x)}` at parse time.\n- **`yin/vm/data.cljc`:** three new exports:\n  - `float64` wraps a value as float64 content.\n  - `float-value` turns it back into a plain host number; this is the execution bridge.\n  - `numeric-key` normalises dict and set keys.\n- **`prelude.cljc`:**\n  - `py/float` wraps through `data/float64` and `py/num` unwraps through `data/float-value`.\n  - Every float constant (13 sites, plus `0.5` and `##Inf`) now goes through `data/float-value`.\n  - Dict-key normalisation uses `data/numeric-key`.\n- **Scalar gates:** `vm.cljc` `plain-data?` and `machine-data?`, `engine.cljc` `scalar?`, and `values.cljc` `kind-of`.\n- **De Bruijn image encoder** (`debruijn_code.cljc` `host-double?`, plus `double-le-hex` there and in `debruijn.cljc`): the ruling did not name this as a gate. Node's stack and register VMs failed with \"Unsupported value in executable scalar encoding\". Both `double-le-hex` copies would also have dropped -0.0's sign on a carrier, so they now unwrap it first.\n- **`render.cljc`:** `float-repr` unwraps the carrier.\n- **Tests:** `prelude_parity_test.cljc` expectations now carry float64 payloads, and `data_test.cljc` gets the updated export set and a new `float-seam-test`.\n- **Docs:** one sentence in `dao.jing.cbor.md`, the normative paragraph (with correction 1) in `yang.antlr.md`, and a scalar-carrier note in the universal-continuation-format doc.\n\n## Decisions and open concerns\n- **Dict-key normalisation (my decision, flagged by fable):** a numeric key is now the integer when it is integral within ±(2^53−1), so 1, 1.0, True and -0.0/0 all collapse to one key. Otherwise the key is float64 content. On the JVM, keys were previously Double 1.0; this needs sign-off. NaN keys equal each other on JS but not on the JVM; that was a host difference before too, and I left it alone.\n- **Bundled addresses have moved on every host** because the prelude changed. No existing test pinned them, so nothing was re-minted, but any external artifacts holding old prelude or A/A' addresses must be rebuilt from source.\n- **Generic yin code is not bridged:** a float literal decoded from a row on JS evaluates as a carrier. Only the Python path converts it back, through `data/float-value`.\n- **No capture inside the seam:** this rests on the existing safepoint test \"the bundled prelude keeps its id: no site is under it\" and on the prelude bodies containing no yield. I added no new test for it.\n- **Not done:** `linker.cljc` `portable-scalar?`, which covers link-request ids, was left unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
